 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 2 gene records and 5 disease records for MLH1, MSH2 and colorectal cancer [7][8][9][10][11][12][13]. \n\nMLH1 encodes a protein that heterodimerizes with PMS2 to form MutL alpha, a core component of the DNA mismatch repair system that introduces single-strand breaks near DNA mismatches [1]. This gene was identified as a locus frequently mutated in hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer [1]. \n\nVariant Evidence and Functional Assays\n\nFunctional assays have been developed to classify MLH1 and MSH2 variants of unknown significance through methylation tolerance testing [2], and disruption of MLH1 C-terminal domain protein interactions confers mismatch repair deficiency [3]. \n\nClinical trials relevant to mismatch repair deficiency include a study of pembrolizumab in microsatellite unstable tumors [4] and trials for genetic cancer risk reduction such as MAGENTA [5] and prophylactic surgery for inherited ovarian cancer risk [6]. \n\nGene records found\n\nGene NCBIGene:4292, name: mutL homolog 1 [7]. Gene NCBIGene:4436, name: mutS homolog 2 [11]. Gene symbol: MLH1 [14]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Muir-Torr\u00c3\u00a9 syndrome [8]. Disease name: Colorectal cancer, hereditary nonpolyposis, type 2 [9]. Disease name: Mismatch repair cancer syndrome 1 [10]. Disease name: Lynch syndrome 1 [12]. Disease name: Mismatch repair cancer syndrome 2 [13]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Functional analysis of human MLH1 variants using yeast and in vitro mismatch repair assays [15]. Pubmed title: Methylation Tolerance-Based Functional Assay to Assess Variants of Unknown Significance in the MLH1 and MSH2 Genes and Identify Patients With Lynch Syndrome [2]. Pubmed title: AI-generated MLH1 small binder improves prime editing efficiency [20]. Pubmed title: Disruption of protein-protein interaction hotspots in the C-terminal domain of MLH1 confers mismatch repair deficiency [3]. Pubmed title: Mismatch repair MLH complexes make distinct contributions to post-replicative mismatch repair versus trinucleotide repeat expansions [23]. \n\nClinvar records found\n\nClinvar title: NM_000249.4(MLH1):c.545+1271_677+737delinsCA [16]. Clinvar title: NM_000249.4(MLH1):c.453+625_545+920delinsT [19]. Clinvar title: NM_000249.4(MLH1):c.1418A>T (p.His473Leu) [21]. Clinvar title: NM_000249.4(MLH1):c.884+5T>A [22]. Clinvar title: NM_000249.4(MLH1):c.1039-1013G>A [24]. \n\nOmim records found\n\nDNA MISMATCH REPAIR PROTEIN MLH1 [17]. MLH1 [17]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: The IMPACT Study - Identification of Men With a Genetic Predisposition to ProstAte Cancer [18]. Clinical trial name: Study of MK-3475 in Patients With Microsatellite Unstable (MSI) Tumors (Cohorts A, B and C) [4]. Clinical trial name: Surgery in Preventing Ovarian Cancer in Patients With Genetic Mutations [6]. Clinical trial name: Stand up to Cancer: MAGENTA (Making Genetic Testing Accessible) [5]. Clinical trial name: Durvalumab and Tremelimumab in Treating Patients With Microsatellite Stable Metastatic Colorectal Cancer to the Liver [25]. \n\nNote: this answer does not address the following entities named in the question: Colorectal cancer. Ask about them individually for a complete answer."
